{\b\fs24 Welcome to D-Island}\par
A Dynamic Island for Windows. It sits at the top centre of your screen and shows what's going on: the time and weather when nothing is, and your music, timers and alerts when something is.\par
\par
{\b Open and close it}\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-240\li360\sa60\sl264\slmult1 Hover over the island (or click it) to open it. Move the pointer away to close it.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Right-click the island to open {\b Settings}. You can also double-click the D-Island icon in the taskbar's notification area, or use {\b D-Island Settings} in the Start menu.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Press {\b Ctrl+Alt+I} to hide or show the island from anywhere.\par
\pard\sa80\sl264\slmult1\par
{\b Pages}\par
Scroll with the mouse wheel or touchpad over the open island, or click the icons along its top, to switch pages:\par
\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-240\li360\sa60\sl264\slmult1{\b Home}: what's playing (works with Spotify, Apple Music, browsers and more), quick timers and a stopwatch.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\b Notifications}: other apps' notifications. Click one to open its app.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\b Calendar}: this month and your next events, with a {\b Join} button for online meetings.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\b Shelf}: drag files onto the island to keep them handy, then drag them out wherever you need them.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\b Weather}: now, the next hours and the next days.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\b Apps}: your favourite apps, one click away (choose them in Settings).\par
\pard\sa80\sl264\slmult1\par
{\b Alerts}\par
The island briefly shows volume and brightness, battery and charging, devices connecting (with earbud battery), Caps Lock, copied text, Do Not Disturb, Wi-Fi and more. An orange or green dot beside it means an app is using your microphone or camera. When two things run at once, for example a timer and music, the second appears as a small bubble beside the island.\par
\par
{\b Notifications and your Windows calendar}\par
Windows only shares these with apps that complete a one-time setup. You can choose it on the next page, or later in {\b Settings > Notifications}. Calendars from Google, Outlook or iCloud can also be added as a link in {\b Settings > Calendar}, with no setup.\par
\par
{\b Make it yours}\par
Settings has the shape (island or notch), material (including Liquid Glass), size, colours, fonts, alarms, and which alerts and pages to show. Everything applies straight away.\par
\par
{\i You can read this again any time: Start menu > How to use D-Island.}\par
